# An operation as a mapping in one record, tied to its inputs by rules in the record that holds
# the products. Mul is exclusive or on 0 and 1: its text is the two inputs, and out is
# computed. Assoc holds four Muls and says what each one takes: 'ab_takes_a_and_b' needs the
# inputs of ab to be the a and b of this Assoc, so 'ab.out' is a times b. A path has one dot,
# so Assoc names each product ('ab_out') before a rule reads it.
#
# An Assoc is eleven digits, with no newline at the end: a, b and c, then the inputs of ab, bc,
# (ab)c and a(bc). Exclusive or is associative, so every text whose products fit their rules
# is accepted (xor.txt); a product that does not fit is refused, naming its rule
# (xor_bc.txt, xor_ab_c.txt).
Element {Integer} value: 0..1
  1. {Digit}

Bit {0 | 1}

Mul {}
  left {Element}
  right {Element}
  out {Bit} (left, right)
    {0} if: left = 0 & right = 0
    {1} if: left = 0 & right = 1
    {1} if: left = 1 & right = 0
    {0} if: left = 1 & right = 1

Assoc {}
  a {Element}
  b {Element}
  c {Element}
  ab {Mul}
  bc {Mul}
  ab_out {Integer} value: ab.out
  bc_out {Integer} value: bc.out
  ab_c {Mul}
  a_bc {Mul}
  ab_takes_a_and_b {Needs} ab.left = a & ab.right = b
  bc_takes_b_and_c {Needs} bc.left = b & bc.right = c
  ab_c_takes_ab_out_and_c {Needs} ab_c.left = ab_out & ab_c.right = c
  a_bc_takes_a_and_bc_out {Needs} a_bc.left = a & a_bc.right = bc_out
  same {Needs} ab_c.out = a_bc.out
